  <subsection xml:id="subsec-309-LearningTargets">
    <title>Learning Targets (LTs)</title>

    <p>
      Here is a list of the 22 LTs for this course. Most will only be referred to by number 
      (as in, "This week's quiz will cover LTs #12, 13 and 14.") so you will need to refer to this list frequently.
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          State the definitions of rational and irrational numbers; and use them along with other tools to prove that a given number is rational or irrational.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Prove properties of rational and irrational numbers, or provide counterexamples to show a property does not hold.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Perform various operations and solve problems involving rational and real numbers and their decimal expansions.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Classify a rational number as having a decimal expansion which is finite, simple-periodic, or delayed-periodic; and use properties of the three types to solve problems.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Perform basic operations on complex numbers, including addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, conjugation, finding the modulus and real/imaginary parts; and use them to prove algebraic properties of complex numbers.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Find and use the polar form of a complex number and the distance formula to prove properties of complex numbers, including using equations to represent objects in the complex plane.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          State Euler's formula and DeMoivre's formula; and use them to solve problems and write proofs.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          State the definition of an isometry; and use it to prove results about isometries and congruence.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Calculate the images of objects under translations; and use definitions and established properties to prove results about translations.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Calculate the images of objects under rotations; and use definitions and established properties to prove results about rotations.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          State the Two Reflection Theorem; and use it to solve problems, including writing a composition of two reflections as a single isometry and writing an isometry as a composition of two reflections.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Calculate the images of objects under reflections and glide reflections; and use definitions and established properties to prove results about reflections and glide reflections.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Identify the type of a given isometry using properties such as fixed points, orientation-preservation (or lack thereof), and the parity of the number of reflections in a composition.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Find equations of congruent graphs formed using various isometries; and solve problems involving them.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          State, prove, and use results from traditional congruence, such as the side-side-side criterion.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Use the taxi-cab metric to calculate distance and prove properties, including connections with the standard Euclidean distance and isometries.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          State the definition of a similarity transformation; and use it to prove results about similarity transformations and similarity.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Find equations of similar graphs formed using various similarity transformations.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Prove facts about similar polygons; and solve problems involving them.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Prove properties and solve problems relating to angle measure and basic right triangle trigonometry.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Prove trigonometric identities, including the Law of Sines and Law of Cosines; and use them to solve problems involving triangles.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          State the definition of area; and use it to find the area of various polygonal regions, including justifying classic formulas for the areas of rectangles, triangles, parallelograms, trapezoids, and circles.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    
  </subsection>
</section>
